\begin{abstract}
Vision-language-action (VLA) models built on large vision-language models (VLMs) generalize well semantically, but their pre-training on high-level tasks such as visual question answering leaves them weak on the low-level spatial and dynamics cues that precise manipulation needs. Prediction-based policies, trained to generate future frames, show the opposite profile: good spatial grounding, little semantic robustness. We present UP-VLA, a single autoregressive model that is trained jointly for multi-modal understanding and future visual prediction and then finetuned to generate actions. Unified prompts and attention masks let continuous CLIP tokens for understanding and discrete VQ-GAN tokens for prediction share one Phi-1.5 backbone, and a joint prediction-and-understanding mechanism conditions actions on a generated scene description and on predicted future image tokens. On CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, UP-VLA completes 4.08 tasks in a row on average versus 3.06 for GR-1, a gain of about 33\%; on ABCD$\rightarrow$D it reaches 4.42 versus 4.21. On a Franka Panda, it improves generalization to unseen objects and precision tasks. Ablations show that the two objectives contribute different capabilities: prediction supports spatial and dynamics grounding, and understanding supports semantic generalization.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Large VLMs are an attractive initialization for robot policies because their web-scale pre-training supplies open-world semantic knowledge. RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt}, OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla} and $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi_0} turn such models into vision-language-action (VLA) policies, and robot data collections \citep{o2023open} make it possible to train them across embodiments. Yet VLMs are pre-trained mostly on understanding tasks such as VQA, which emphasise \emph{what} is in the image rather than depth, size, relative pose or how a scene will change. Prior studies document such gaps in spatial relations \citep{chen2024spatialvlm,wen2024can}, physical dynamics \citep{balazadeh2024synthetic,ghaffari2024exploring} and low-level visual features \citep{zheng2024lm4lv}. Precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes depend on exactly these cues.

A second family of policies pre-trains on prediction: it learns to generate future frames or sub-goal images from video and robot data, as in GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing}, SuSIE \citep{black2023zero} and Uni-Pi \citep{du2024learning}. Predicting the future forces a model to represent geometry and motion, but the resulting policies are trained on comparatively narrow robot data and lack the semantic grounding that VLMs provide. \cref{fig:teaser,fig:compare} contrast the two families with UP-VLA: VLM-initialized VLAs excel at semantic generalization but struggle with fine control, while prediction-oriented policies show the converse.

