We argue the two should be trained together. UP-VLA is an autoregressive VLA that learns multi-modal understanding (MMU) from large-scale image-text data and future visual prediction (PRE) from robot video and action data in the same backbone. The difficulty is that the two objectives want different inputs: understanding is conventionally done on continuous semantic image features, while autoregressive image generation works on discrete tokens, with a different attention pattern. We resolve this with task-specific prompts and attention masks, and at action time we condition the policy on both a scene description produced by the model itself and the predicted future.

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item UP-VLA, a unified VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding and future prediction in one autoregressive framework.
\item A unified prompting and attention-masking strategy that mixes continuous MMU tokens and discrete predictive tokens in a single LLM (\cref{sec:method}).
\item A joint prediction-and-understanding action mechanism that conditions actions on both a semantic scene description and predicted futures.
\item State-of-the-art results on CALVIN (ABC$\rightarrow$D average length 4.08, about 33\% above GR-1), real-world gains in semantic generalization and precision, and ablations that separate the roles of the two objectives (\cref{sec:exp}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Works}
\paragraph{VLM-based VLA models.}
Language-conditioned robot learning has progressed from instruction-following with small models \citep{stepputtis2020language,lynch2020grounding,jang2022bc} to transformer policies such as RT-1 \citep{brohan2022rt} and CLIPort \citep{shridhar2022cliport}, and then to large pre-trained models used as planners \citep{ahn2022can,huang2022inner,liang2023code} or as policy backbones. PaLM-E \citep{driess2023palm} and RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt} fine-tune multimodal models on robot data; later work adds action hierarchies \citep{belkhale2024rt}, hierarchical transformers \citep{zhang2024hirt}, visual trace prompting \citep{zheng2024tracevla}, an open-source recipe \citep{kim24openvla}, a flow-matching action head \citep{black2024pi_0}, and 3D inputs with a generative world model \citep{zhen20243d}. RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision} adapts a VLM into a manipulation imitator. These models inherit VLM semantics, but their pre-training does not ask them to model how a scene evolves. UP-VLA keeps an MMU objective for semantics and adds an explicit future-prediction objective.

\paragraph{Prediction-based policies.}
A separate line uses generative models of the future as the source of control signals. SuSIE \citep{black2023zero} fine-tunes an image-editing diffusion model to propose sub-goal images for a low-level controller; Uni-Pi \citep{du2024learning} and video language planning \citep{du2023video} generate videos from text; GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing} pre-trains a GPT-style model on large-scale video and predicts future images and actions end to end; PAD \citep{guo2024prediction} denoises images and actions jointly, and GEVRM \citep{zhang2025gevrm} generates goal videos for robust manipulation. IGOR \citep{chen2024igor} learns image-goal representations as control units. Diffusion-based policies \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy,ke20243d,reuss2024multimodal} model action distributions directly. These methods gain spatial grounding but are not initialised from, or co-trained with, language understanding. UP-VLA differs by performing prediction \emph{and} understanding in one language-model backbone.

\paragraph{Unified understanding and generation.}
In vision-language modelling, several works train one model for both comprehension and generation: Show-o \citep{xie2024show} unifies them in a single transformer, SEED-X \citep{ge2024seed} and DreamLLM \citep{dong2023dreamllm} combine multimodal comprehension with creation, and autoregressive models over discrete image tokens \citep{esser2021taming,sun2024autoregressive,yu2023magvit} or video tokens \citep{kondratyuk2023videopoet} show that language-model machinery can generate visual content. We borrow this unification for embodied control, where the generated image is a prediction of the future and the model must also output actions.

\paragraph{Spatial and physical limits of VLMs.}
SpatialVLM \citep{chen2024spatialvlm} reports that VLMs lack spatial reasoning and trains on synthetic spatial data to remedy it; related analyses examine object relations \citep{wen2024can}, physical dynamics \citep{balazadeh2024synthetic,ghaffari2024exploring} and low-level vision \citep{zheng2024lm4lv}. These works motivate our design, but target VQA-style evaluation rather than robot control; we instead use future prediction on robot data as the source of low-level supervision.

\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:prelim}
We consider language-conditioned manipulation: given an instruction $L$ and the observation $O_t$ at time $t$, a policy outputs a chunk of low-level actions $a_{t:t+\Delta t}$. Two sources of supervision are available: image-text data for understanding, and robot demonstrations containing observations, instructions and actions. UP-VLA builds on a pre-trained language model, Phi-1.5 \citep{li2023textbooks}, with two visual pathways. A frozen CLIP-ViT \citep{radford2021learning} followed by an MLP projection $E_1$ maps an image into continuous tokens $u$ in the language embedding space, as in LLaVA-style models \citep{liu2024visual}. A frozen VQ-GAN encoder $E_2$ \citep{esser2021taming} maps an image to discrete codebook indices $v$, on which future prediction is performed; a VQ decoder reconstructs images from predicted tokens. All encoders are frozen and the LLM is fully fine-tuned.

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:method}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{figures/fig3_v3.png}
\caption{Overview of UP-VLA.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}
\cref{fig:overview} gives an overview. UP-VLA has one backbone, two image pathways and three tasks (understanding, prediction, action), and is trained in two stages.

\subsection{Unified prompting and attention masks}
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4.png}
\caption{Illustration of the unified prompting and attention mechanism.}
\label{fig:prompt}
\end{figure}
Each task has its own task token, input layout and attention mask (\cref{fig:prompt}).

\emph{Multi-modal understanding (MMU).} The input is $\{[\text{MMU}], u, l\}$: the continuous image tokens $u$ come before the text $l$, so that image tokens can attend to each other, and the model predicts the next language tokens, $\hat{L}=\pi_\theta^{\text{MMU}}(I,L)$.

\emph{Future prediction (PRE).} The input is $\{[\text{PRE}], l, v_t\}$: the language tokens come first and the discrete tokens of the current image $v_t$ follow, so that image tokens attend to all earlier tokens. The model predicts the tokens of the future image at the aligned positions rather than as a next-token continuation of the sequence, $\hat{O}_{t+\Delta t}=\pi_\theta^{\text{PRE}}(O_t,L)$.

\emph{Action learning.} For actions, the model concatenates an MMU-driven scene description and the current visual tokens with the instruction, so that action tokens attend both to semantic (MMU) tokens and to the visual tokens used for prediction.

\subsection{Joint prediction and understanding for action}
Given $(O_t, L)$, UP-VLA first generates a scene description $s=\pi_\theta^{\text{MMU}}(O_t, L_{\text{prompt}})$ with a prompt such as ``describe this image'', embeds additional visual information $E_1(O'_t)$ into the language space, and forms the extended instruction $L'=[E_1(O'_t), s, L]$. It then jointly predicts future image tokens and actions,
\begin{equation}
(\hat{H}_{O_{t+\Delta t}}, \hat{H}_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}})=\pi_\theta^{\text{PRE}}(O_t, L'),
\end{equation}
where $\hat H$ denotes internal output tokens. A lightweight policy head (a single-layer attention pooling, MAP, followed by an MLP) maps the action tokens to continuous low-level actions, $\hat a_{t:t+\Delta t}=\text{MLP}(\text{MAP}(\hat H_{A_{t:t+\Delta t}}))$. The scene description gives the policy explicit semantics, while the predicted future gives it spatial and dynamics information.

\subsection{Training objectives}
The MMU loss is the language-modelling cross-entropy over next text tokens, $\mathcal{L}_{\text{MMU}}=-\sum_i \log p_\theta(l_i\mid u, l_{1:i-1})$. The prediction loss is a cross-entropy over the $M$ future image tokens, with $P(O_{t+\Delta t}\mid O_t,L)=\prod_{j=1}^{M} p_\theta(v'_j\mid l, v_{1:M})$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\text{PRE}}=-\sum_j \log p_\theta(v'_j\mid l, v_{1:M})$. The action loss combines a squared error on the continuous action components with a binary cross-entropy on the gripper-end signal, $\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACT}}=\lVert\hat a_{\text{pos}}-a_{\text{pos}}\rVert_2^2+\text{BCE}(\hat a_{\text{end}},a_{\text{end}})$. The total loss is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}=\lambda_1\mathcal{L}_{\text{MMU}}+\lambda_2\mathcal{L}_{\text{PRE}}+\lambda_3\mathcal{L}_{\text{ACT}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Training pipeline and inference}
Stage 1 pre-trains on a mixture of MMU data (LLaVA-tuning-665k, \citealp{liu2024visual}) and PRE data (25k demonstrations from Bridge, \citealp{walke2023bridgedata}), each with its task-specific prompt and mask. Stage 2 finetunes for actions on downstream robot data with joint prediction-and-understanding prompts, continuing MMU co-training to retain semantic ability. At test time, the model receives $O_t$ and $L$, generates $s$, builds $L'$, runs the PRE pathway to obtain future tokens and action tokens, decodes actions through MAP+MLP, executes them and repeats.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:exp}
\subsection{Setup}
\textbf{Simulation.} We use CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin}, a benchmark for long-horizon language-conditioned manipulation with four scenes A, B, C and D. In ABC$\rightarrow$D the policy is trained on three scenes and evaluated zero-shot on the fourth; in ABCD$\rightarrow$D it is trained on all four. The agent must complete five chained tasks, and we report the success rate at each position in the chain and the average number of tasks completed in a row (Avg.\ Len).
\textbf{Real world.} We use a Franka Emika Panda with more than 2k demonstrations over 6 skills, and evaluate seen tasks, grasping of unseen objects and precise operations such as cable routing and small-object grasping, with 20 trials per task (\cref{fig:env}).
\textbf{Baselines.} VLA-based: RoboFlamingo \citep{li2023vision}, 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d}, and UP-VLA-RT-2, our implementation in the style of RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt}. Prediction-based: GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing}, SuSIE \citep{black2023zero}, Uni-Pi \citep{du2024learning}. Others: 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d}, Diffusion Policy \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy} and RT-1 \citep{brohan2022rt}. UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu is a GR-1-like model trained with the same Phi-1.5 backbone but without the MMU objective. Entries marked with * in the tables are our reproductions; other numbers are copied from the original papers.

