\cref{table2} shows ABCD$\rightarrow$D, where all scenes are seen in training. UP-VLA again leads with 4.42, versus 4.21 for GR-1 and 4.09 for RoboFlamingo. The margin is smaller than in ABC$\rightarrow$D (0.21 versus 1.02 over GR-1), and RoboFlamingo has the best first-task success (0.964); the benefit of UP-VLA is largest where generalization to a new scene is needed.

\subsection{Real-world results}
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig6.png}
\caption{Results on real-world manipulation tasks (success rate, 20 trials per task) on seen tasks, unseen objects and unseen precise tasks.}
\label{fig:real}
\end{figure}
\cref{fig:real} summarises the real-robot results. On seen tasks, the LLM-backbone methods (UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu at 0.75, UP-VLA-RT-2 at 0.65 and UP-VLA at 0.80) all outperform RT-1 (0.40) and Diffusion Policy (0.30). On grasping unseen objects, UP-VLA-RT-2 (0.50) is better than UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu (0.30), consistent with the semantic knowledge retained from a VLM, and UP-VLA is best (0.60). On precise tasks the order of the two ablated variants reverses, with UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu (0.35) above UP-VLA-RT-2 (0.20), consistent with the benefit of future prediction for spatial detail, and UP-VLA is best again (0.55). Hence UP-VLA is the only variant that is strong in both semantic and precision settings. With 20 trials per task, differences of one or two successes (0.05--0.10) should not be over-interpreted; we report no confidence intervals or seed variance because they are not available.

\subsection{Ablation study}
\input{tables/table_yn.tex}
\cref{table:ablate} removes one component at a time (full model: Avg.\ Len 4.08 on ABC$\rightarrow$D, 0.80 real seen, 0.58 real unseen). \emph{Without prediction}, simulation falls to 1.44 and both real-world numbers drop (0.65 and 0.35), so prediction is crucial for spatial and dynamics grounding. \emph{Without Bridge pre-training}, simulation falls to 2.74 and real-world numbers to 0.65 and 0.30, so predictive pre-training on 25k Bridge demonstrations boosts downstream performance. \emph{Without MMU}, simulation is only slightly lower (3.89) and seen-task success is even higher (0.85), but unseen-object success drops to 0.20 from 0.58, indicating that MMU is critical for semantic generalization. \emph{Without MMU-condition}, i.e.\ without conditioning actions on the self-generated scene description, performance is 3.99 / 0.80 / 0.50, smaller but consistent drops in simulation and on unseen objects, so the conditioning itself helps in addition to MMU co-training.

\subsection{Qualitative analysis}
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig7.png}
\caption{Visualization of VQA results and predicted future images.}
\label{fig:vis}
