\cref{fig:vis} shows that UP-VLA identifies objects and their approximate relative positions in VQA, which helps ground its actions, and that its predicted future images show robot and object motion consistent with the instruction. The model also makes mistakes: it occasionally misidentifies objects, which we attribute to the backbone and data scale, and its predictions show background colour artifacts in unseen scenes (D versus A--C), suggesting limited generative generalization.

\section{Limitations}
The generative artifacts in unseen scenes indicate that visual generation is insufficiently pre-trained; better tokenizers or diffusion-based modules may help. MMU occasionally misclassifies objects, reflecting the limits of the backbone and data scale; larger VLMs and richer curated data could reduce such errors. Joint training lengthens sequences and adds training cost, which parameter-efficient finetuning or structured memory might reduce. Our evidence has further limits: real-world results use 20 trials per task without reported variance, some baselines are copied from the original papers and others reproduced, and we do not report the loss weights or training hyperparameters. Finally, UP-VLA uses 2D images; extending it to depth, point clouds, multiple views or explicit physics is left to future work.

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding and future visual prediction in one autoregressive VLA and conditions action generation on a generated scene description and predicted future tokens. It reaches an average length of 4.08 on CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D and 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D, and improves real-world unseen-object and precision tasks; the ablations attribute spatial and dynamics grounding to prediction and semantic generalization to understanding. These results come from one benchmark and a small real-robot study, so the evidence for broader generalist-policy claims remains limited.

